            {
              "id": "8b78fa50-ae70-4b8c-9983-771202eca613",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<em><strong>The Play of Man</strong></em><em> </em>was written by <a href=\"#/karl-groos\">Karl Groos</a>, originally published in 1899."
            },
            {
              "id": "7f0e2a23-c249-45c8-8e06-bc961758c4b8",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            },
            {
              "id": "b044f366-ec1c-4cad-a30f-e8d048089665",
              "type": "quote",
              "html": "<em>The Play of Man is a work by Karl Groos, philosopher and psychologist who proposed an </em><a href=\"#/evolution\"><em>evolutionary</em></a><em> </em><a href=\"#/instrument\"><em>instrumentalist</em></a><em> theory of play. The book suggests that play is a preparation for later life. The main idea is that play is basically useful, and so it can be explained by the normal process of evolution by natural selection. When we &quot;play&quot; we are practicing basic instincts, such as fighting, for survival, just like animals do.</em>"
            },
            {
              "id": "6600f3b6-2ded-41a0-9dab-0feb245f245c",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            }
          ]
